{"name":"Norfolk Green","hex":"#2e4b3c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Stagecoach in Norfolk","blocks":[{"text":"Stagecoach in Norfolk was a bus operator based in King's Lynn in Norfolk, England. It operated public bus services in the counties of Norfolk, Cambridgeshire and Lincolnshire as well as numerous school and college services. It was a subsidiary of Stagecoach."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Norfolk_Green_bus_502_%28YJ58_VCC%29%2C_Showbus_2009.jpg/330px-Norfolk_Green_bus_502_%28YJ58_VCC%29%2C_Showbus_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stagecoach_in_Norfolk","wikidata":"Q12064348"},{"title":"Norfolk","blocks":[{"text":"Norfolk is a ceremonial county in England, located in the East of England and East Anglia. It borders Lincolnshire and The Wash to the north-west, the North Sea to the north and east, Cambridgeshire to the west, and Suffolk to the south. The largest settlement is the city of Norwich."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Kings_Lynn_Guild_Hall_%28geograph_4626827%29.jpg/330px-Kings_Lynn_Guild_Hall_%28geograph_4626827%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norfolk","wikidata":"Q23109"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Norfolk_Green_21_SN12_EHM.jpg/500px-Norfolk_Green_21_SN12_EHM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Norfolk_Green_21_SN12_EHM.jpg/1280px-Norfolk_Green_21_SN12_EHM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Norfolk_Green_21_SN12_EHM.jpg/500px-Norfolk_Green_21_SN12_EHM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Alexander Dennis Enviro400 in September 2012","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANorfolk_Green_21_SN12_EHM.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Cley_windmill%2C_from_Beach_Road%2C_Cley_next_the_Sea_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3005259_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Cley_windmill%2C_from_Beach_Road%2C_Cley_next_the_Sea_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3005